ble but this  changed over time as the stone became  increasingly seen as a status symbol and  many of the limestone quarries in the  basin started shutting down  limestone is also a strong robust  building material that's relatively easy  to cut and shape  so it was an obvious option for the  buildings that would line houseman's  boulevards  when it came to the designs houseman was  hands off with interior but very  hands-on with exterior  buildings were standardized around six  floors the ground floor used for shops  the first for offices the second for  nobles and the rest of the floors going  up inversely with class with the upper  floors being intended for servants  there are three important features  you'll recognize but not only in paris  intricate wrought iron balconies and  cuboid roofs made of glass and zinc  all three the reflection of the second  empire style the napoleon iii exported  across the world  wrought iron was malleable and able to  resist large tensile forces and it was  used in some of the largest  architectural feats of the second empire  while also being used as park entrances  and balconies  the top floor roof of houseman's  buildings monsard a nearly flat top with  steep sloped sides  designed that way because the top floor  was practically always used as living  space and it's also the reason why  you'll usually see an array of windows  for the roof a zinc alloy a combination  of zinc copper titanium or tin  before houseman most of paris's roofs  were made of slate brought from the  loire valley  but zinc sheets were lighter cheaper  stronger recyclable and easier to  install  and zinc also has the benefit of being  self-healing which helps it last decades  and zinc sheeting can also be bent into  elaborate shapes and the slots you see  on parisian roofs are the result of  these sheets being stapled together  anyway  back to the park  within paris separated between the right  and left banks are the local mines that  were used to collect 